WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:05.880
Water represents one
of the biggest threats

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.523
posed by climate change.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:10.080
That's because rising seas,

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.280
stronger storms,

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:12.410
and heavier rainfall

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:14.130
could massively increase flooding

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:15.433
by the end of the century.

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:18.760
It's already starting to happen

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:21.210
and it's very likely to get worse.

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:22.960
Catastrophic flooding in Western Europe

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:24.150
after violent storms

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:26.963
and powerful flood waters
destroying entire towns.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:29.700
It's easy to feel overwhelmed

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.680
looking at footage like this.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.660
It seems like no one has any idea

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:35.460
what to do about this problem.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:38.750
So, we're just going to see
more and more damaging floods,

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:39.923
year after year.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:44.640
But there is one place where
it's possible to imagine

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:46.193
a very different future.

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:55.320
When you look at the city of Rotterdam,

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:56.700
the water is in our genes,

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:57.590
you could say.

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:01.193
We are already working for
1,000 years on this topic.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:03.160
Keeping the water out

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:05.610
isn't a new challenge for the Netherlands.

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:08.740
It's a battle they've been
fighting for centuries.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:10.310
So, when the effects of climate change

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:11.490
started to crop up,

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:13.330
they were like, yeah, let's do this.

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:14.850
We're ready.

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:16.310
We traveled to Rotterdam

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:19.570
to find out how this city is
getting ready for the future

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:22.220
and whether the ingenious
stuff they've come up with

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:24.150
will ultimately be enough to save them

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:27.643
and hopefully the rest of us, too.

00:01:37.104 --> 00:01:40.410
When you look at Holland
or the Netherlands,

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:41.910
the name, the Netherlands,

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:45.040
already says that we
are a low-lying country.

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:46.660
One-third of this country

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:47.740
is below sea level

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:50.213
and two thirds is vulnerable to flooding.

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:54.290
Back in the day,

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:56.430
the only things keeping the water at bay

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:57.570
were the dikes,

00:01:57.570 --> 00:01:59.970
which are basically just
raised mounds of earth.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:02.810
Once every few decades,

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:04.570
the water would overwhelm the dikes

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:06.760
and cause catastrophic flooding

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:08.683
and that was just the way things were,

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:13.090
until one day in 1953,

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:15.173
all that suddenly changed.

00:02:21.519 --> 00:02:23.852
In '53, it was a disaster.

00:02:40.061 --> 00:02:43.220
A powerful storm struck
the Southern Coast,

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:45.700
overwhelming dikes that
had been badly damaged

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:46.923
in the second World War.

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:54.018
On the morning of February 1st, 1953,

00:02:54.018 --> 00:02:55.360
Koos' father and brother

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:58.943
woke him up and told
him what had happened.

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:15.400
In 1953, almost 2,000 people died

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:16.420
and that was the moment for us,

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:19.050
we said, this can never happen again.

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:21.500
This is time we started
making our first barrier.

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:27.090
The flood galvanized the Dutch government

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:30.670
to build the world's most
powerful flood defense system,

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:31.803
the Delta Works.

00:03:33.010 --> 00:03:34.950
It was a series of modernized dams

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:36.530
and storm surge barriers

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:38.893
that took more than 40 years to complete.

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:42.110
And the final act of the Delta Works

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:43.450
is the barrier over here,

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:44.500
the Maeslant Barrier,

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:46.313
made to protect Rotterdam.

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:50.130
We're looking at the biggest movable

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:52.590
storm surge barrier in the world.

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:54.480
This is the same size as the Eiffel Tower.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:57.280
So, you're looking at the
Eiffel Tower laying on its side.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:02.000
Most of the time,

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:03.240
the barrier stays open,

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:04.593
so ships can pass through.

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:07.490
When a storm comes along,

00:04:07.490 --> 00:04:08.930
it snaps shut,

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:11.690
preventing the storm surge
from traveling up river

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:12.840
and flooding Rotterdam.

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:16.790
The moveable design was radical

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:19.590
when it was completed in 1997

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:21.520
and it's inspired similar structures

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:23.593
in flood-prone cities around the world.

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:27.130
If you look at St. Petersburg in Russia,

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:30.590
a small version of this barrier
has been made over there

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:31.900
and maybe in the future,

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:33.120
in front of Manhattan,

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:35.583
they're thinking about a
way to protect that, also.

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:38.060
So, this was the first one

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:39.590
and now smaller versions

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:41.090
are coming all over the world.

00:04:44.003 --> 00:04:45.500
But the barrier on its own

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:48.363
isn't enough to keep
Rotterdam from flooding.

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:55.530
Talking about climate change,

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:59.193
we see an increase of
more intensive rainfall.

00:05:02.170 --> 00:05:04.370
We already are facing now and then,

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:06.130
small-scale flooding.

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:07.810
So, we need more places

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:09.903
where we can store this water.

00:05:13.814 --> 00:05:14.900
Arnoud Molenaar

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:17.950
is also working to keep Rotterdam dry,

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:19.890
not by keeping the water out,

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:21.513
but by giving it a place to go.

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:25.150
This is the water square

00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:26.830
and it is actually, you could say,

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:29.120
a symbol for our approach,

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:31.103
how to become climate adaptive.

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:34.810
So, under normal circumstances,

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:37.210
this square can be used as,

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:38.810
for example, a basketball field.

00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:41.950
But on days like this,

00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:43.780
it stores excess rainwater

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:45.730
and keeps it from flooding the streets.

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:49.520
So, it's a multifunctional solution.

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:52.460
We want to add quality to the urban space,

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:53.300
but in this way,

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:55.853
we are also adding water storage.

00:05:58.350 --> 00:05:59.780
This is the first water square

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:01.630
that we have been developing,

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:04.633
but it's part of a bigger
package of measures.

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:07.047
An interesting one close by here is

00:06:07.047 --> 00:06:08.820
what we call the dakpark

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:11.170
or the rooftop park,

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:13.250
which is a kind of a long levy

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:14.760
of one kilometer

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:17.513
and on top of it, a huge green park.

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:20.580
It's storing excessive rain water,

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:21.880
because of the green roof.

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:27.240
Looking at the challenges
related to climate change,

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:29.780
a lot of people think
it's going to cost us

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:31.880
a lot of space and money

00:06:31.880 --> 00:06:34.890
and we try to show that
you also can use it

00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:36.253
to work on a better city.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:43.920
In some ways,

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:46.380
the Dutch are just as
baffled as the rest of us

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:47.693
about climate change.

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:51.383
For one thing,

00:06:51.383 --> 00:06:54.070
there's the question of what
happens when sea levels rise

00:06:54.070 --> 00:06:55.220
beyond a certain point?

00:06:57.330 --> 00:06:59.480
The Maeslant Barrier
was built to withstand

00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:01.860
one meter of sea level rise

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:03.140
and a redesigned barrier

00:07:03.140 --> 00:07:06.240
could potentially handle
even more than that,

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:09.413
but there's only so much that
even Dutch engineering can do.

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:11.870
Beyond two meters,

00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:12.913
it's difficult.

00:07:14.170 --> 00:07:16.560
We are working on new scenarios,

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:19.500
suppose it will be more than two meters,

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:20.650
four meters, five meters,

00:07:20.650 --> 00:07:21.680
then what?

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:22.580
People ask me,

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:25.490
will you be moving on a certain

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:28.419
moment in time

00:07:28.419 --> 00:07:30.478
to the higher grounds in the Netherlands?

00:07:30.478 --> 00:07:33.413
I can't imagine that
that will be a decision.

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:36.410
For survivors like Koos,

00:07:36.410 --> 00:07:37.370
on the other hand,

00:07:37.370 --> 00:07:40.537
it's not so hard to imagine the worst.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:54.561
I think in 300 years,

00:07:54.561 --> 00:07:56.673
Rotterdam will still be here,

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:00.990
but when the sea level will
rise four or five meters,

00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:04.290
we will have to find additional solutions.

00:08:04.290 --> 00:08:06.093
Any idea what those would be?

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:08.250
Maybe on a larger scale,

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:10.740
invest in floating districts,

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:11.773
floating cities.

00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:15.770
And that brings us to our final

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:17.723
and most radical solution.

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:20.360
My name is Koen Olthuis.

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:22.500
I'm a water architect.

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:23.600
For the last 15 years,

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:25.360
We've been building floating homes

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:27.410
in cities that face climate change

00:08:27.410 --> 00:08:28.360
and sea level rise.

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:33.840
So, we have the floating home,

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:35.660
which has a floating foundation

00:08:35.660 --> 00:08:37.090
and it can move up and down

00:08:37.090 --> 00:08:39.020
with the fluctuation of the river,

00:08:39.020 --> 00:08:40.070
but to keep it on one spot,

00:08:40.070 --> 00:08:41.920
we have these kind of stilts.

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:42.753
The water will come up

00:08:42.753 --> 00:08:44.930
and you will see that the
house will move up, up, up, up

00:08:44.930 --> 00:08:46.470
and if the water goes down,

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:47.793
it just guides it back.

00:08:49.590 --> 00:08:50.423
100 years ago,

00:08:50.423 --> 00:08:53.130
you see cities start to grow
up with high-rise buildings,

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:54.800
because they saw the space in the air.

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:57.760
Well, we see the city
has to grow in the water.

00:08:57.760 --> 00:08:59.090
It's not only about houses.

00:08:59.090 --> 00:09:00.690
It's about floating apartment buildings,

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:01.560
floating roads,

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:02.670
floating parks,

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:04.820
all the kind of urban components you need

00:09:04.820 --> 00:09:07.860
to make your city more waterproof.

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:09.100
Who knows,

00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:12.070
maybe someday, we'll all
live in floating houses

00:09:12.070 --> 00:09:14.513
or maybe something entirely
new will come along.

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:18.460
Preparing for climate
change is a tough challenge,

00:09:18.460 --> 00:09:20.360
but for the innovators of Rotterdam,

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:23.103
it's a process of
adaptation that never ends.

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:25.840
It's not finished, our project.

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:26.673
It keeps on going.

00:09:26.673 --> 00:09:27.883
It's an ongoing process.

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:29.910
I feel safe.

00:09:29.910 --> 00:09:31.150
My children will feel safe

00:09:31.150 --> 00:09:33.597
and my grandchildren will also feel safe,

00:09:33.597 --> 00:09:35.863
but after that, you have to think again.

